    "description": "Land use management in Iran connects the classification of land with public land allocation, urban development and protection of agricultural areas. Understanding it requires distinguishing a parcel’s legal history from its planned use and its access to infrastructure.",
    "tags": ["Land use", "Urban land", "Planning", "Property", "Infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:25:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:25:28Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Land Use Management in Iran: Classification and Development",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s urban land classifications, the relationship between land allocation and infrastructure, and the limits of historical legal evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "classification",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A parcel has more than one identity",
        "content": "Iran’s Urban Land Law distinguishes land according to its history of development and use. In the inspected Persian text, *mavat* refers to land without a history of development or reclamation; *bayer* describes previously developed land that has subsequently fallen out of use; and *dayer* describes land that has been developed and remains in use. The statutory definition of dayer land for this law specifically concerns agricultural or fallow land. These are legal classifications, rather than interchangeable descriptions of an empty plot. [s1]\n\nThe distinction matters because the law attaches different consequences to different categories. Its provisions address state control of mavat land, land needed for housing and public services, and the preparation and allocation of public land. A visible absence of buildings is therefore insufficient to establish a parcel’s legal category. Historical use and the applicable legal determination matter. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "land-and-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Allocation is only part of development",
        "content": "The World Bank’s 2004 housing-sector study described public land acquisition and distribution as central features of Iran’s post-revolutionary housing policy. It also documented a practical problem: distributing land did not necessarily deliver serviced neighborhoods. Its discussion of new towns identified difficulties financing trunk infrastructure even where developers had already acquired land. This is historical evidence, not a verified description of every new town today. [s2]\n\nThe distinction provides a useful way to read development announcements. Land allocation, infrastructure provision and usable housing are separate achievements. Reporting should identify which stage has actually been completed rather than treating a parcel allocation as evidence that a functioning neighborhood exists. This is an editorial inference from the implementation problems documented in the study. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "agricultural-boundary",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The urban edge changes the question",
        "content": "The inspected reproduction of the agricultural-land protection law treats conversion of agricultural land and gardens outside the legal boundaries of cities and towns as a restricted activity, with an exception procedure for necessary cases. It also directs urban expansion toward non-agricultural land where possible. This establishes an important historical distinction between urban land administration and protection of productive land beyond urban boundaries. [s3]\n\nThe reproduction is an excerpt hosted by a private legal website. It does not establish the complete consolidated rules applicable in October 2026. Current boundaries, amendments and parcel-specific decisions require verification before describing any particular conversion as authorized. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
