    "description": "Housing in Iran encompasses the production of dwellings, access to ownership and rental accommodation, and the quality of residential neighborhoods. Historical research and international assessments show why construction, finance, land and household circumstances must be examined together.",
    "tags": ["Housing", "Homeownership", "Rental housing", "Construction", "Affordability", "Urban development"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:25:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:25:28Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Housing in Iran: Homes, Tenure and Affordability",
      "description": "Explore the historical development of housing in Iran and the connections between construction, rental access, finance and adequate living conditions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "historical-development",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From housing production to housing conditions",
        "content": "Iran’s housing history involves both expanding the supply of dwellings and changing how housing is organized. Habibollah Zanjani’s Encyclopaedia Iranica account traces housing’s emergence as a distinct development-planning subject during the third development plan of the 1960s. Private investment played a prominent role, while the state developed institutions and regulatory frameworks. Housing policy therefore cannot be understood solely through government construction projects. [s1]\n\nThe same account distinguishes housing units from households. Several households can occupy one dwelling, so counting households as if each already had a separate home can conceal crowding or shared accommodation. Historical censuses provided a basis for examining that difference. The article’s evidence primarily concerns the twentieth century and should not be presented as a current inventory. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "connected-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The branches of the housing question",
        "content": "This section separates market conditions, rental housing, construction, social housing, finance and industrialized building so that different questions can be examined on their own terms. The connections are equally important. The World Bank’s 2004 sector study distinguished construction finance from long-term purchase finance and argued that housing-finance reform needed to be connected to land use, construction technology and subsidy policy. Those were the study’s diagnoses and recommendations at the time, rather than proof that its proposed reforms were subsequently implemented. [s2]\n\nThat distinction remains useful when reading policy documents: a proposal, a financing allocation and a completed dwelling describe different stages. The relevant evidence must establish the stage being claimed.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "adequacy-and-inclusion",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A home is more than a completed building",
        "content": "The UN Special Rapporteur on adequate housing visited Iran in 2005 and reported in 2006. His assessment acknowledged efforts to extend basic services and reconstruct housing in Bam, while identifying obstacles to affordable housing and access to credit for very poor households. It also raised concerns about insecure tenure, discrimination and services in informal settlements. These are attributed findings from a historical mission, not a newly measured account of conditions in 2026. [s3]\n\nThe report helps explain why housing assessment needs to examine who can obtain a dwelling, under what tenure arrangements, and with which services. A construction total alone cannot answer those questions. Its concerns about coordination between responsible institutions also show why a national policy commitment does not by itself establish a local outcome. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "affordability-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The affordability constraint",
        "content": "The World Bank’s Spring 2024 Iran Economic Monitor described limited financing and rising production costs as constraints on construction. It also linked rising housing prices with reduced access to ownership and higher rental costs. These findings connect the supply side of housing with household purchasing power: producing homes and enabling people to occupy them affordably are related but separate challenges. [s4]\n\nA current national assessment still requires recent evidence on tenure, completions, rents, household incomes and regional differences. Until those sources are reconciled, this page provides a supported historical and analytical introduction rather than a complete account of housing conditions at the editorial cutoff.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
