    "description": "Iran’s housing market must be examined through local property characteristics, transaction evidence and household affordability. Tehran research provides useful insights, but its findings cannot automatically describe markets throughout the country.",
    "tags": ["Housing market", "Tehran", "Property prices", "Affordability", "Housing data"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:25:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:25:28Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Housing Market: Prices, Location and Evidence",
      "description": "Read Iran’s housing-market evidence carefully: distinguish local property characteristics, model estimates, transaction prices and household affordability."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "local-property-differences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why location and dwelling characteristics matter",
        "content": "Research on Tehran illustrates why a housing market cannot be reduced to a single citywide price. A 2024 study by Ali Asghar Pilehvar and Arian Ghasemi examined relationships between residential property values and characteristics including floor area, neighborhood, building age, parking and elevators. Its reported correlations underline the importance of comparing similar properties. They do not establish that adding an amenity would independently cause the corresponding price change. [s1]\n\nThe distinction between total property value and price per unit of floor area is especially important. Larger homes can have higher total values without necessarily being more expensive per square meter. A comparison also needs to account for the location and characteristics of the properties represented. These are methodological implications of the study’s focus on heterogeneous dwellings, rather than a new estimate of Tehran prices. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "models-and-observations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A fitted price is not a completed sale",
        "content": "A Tehran housing-price preprint submitted in December 2021 compared statistical and machine-learning methods using a historical transaction dataset. The author evaluated predictions against a held-out portion of that dataset and reported stronger performance for a random-forest model under the study’s selected criterion. The inspected abstract supports that narrow description of the exercise; it does not establish how the model would perform in later market conditions. [s2]\n\nThis matters when interpreting property dashboards. A model output estimates a value under its assumptions and training data. It should be labeled separately from an observed transaction. Similarly, success at estimating prices within a historical sample does not establish reliable forecasts of future market movements. The preprint is useful as an example of prediction research, not as a current valuation service. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "affordability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Prices need a household context",
        "content": "The World Bank’s Spring 2024 economic assessment linked rising housing prices to difficulties accessing ownership and to higher rents. It also identified high mortgage costs and cumbersome loan procedures as constraints. This suggests that affordability analysis must examine financing access alongside property prices; a price observation alone cannot show whether a household can purchase a home. [s3]\n\nThe geographical scope of the evidence also matters. Tehran-specific observations should retain that label. They cannot establish national price levels or the circumstances of households in other cities and rural areas.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-needed",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains to be established",
        "content": "A market assessment at the editorial cutoff needs a documented series with consistent geographical coverage, reporting periods and property definitions. It also needs transaction volumes and appropriate household-income evidence before drawing conclusions about affordability. The sources inspected here do not supply that complete combination. Consequently, this page offers an evidence-based guide to interpreting market claims, while withholding a current price estimate, annual chart or forecast.",
        "source_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
